Polling subscriptions for @parcel/watcher's brute-force backend.

Upstream's BruteForceBackend only implements snapshots (writeSnapshot /
getEventsSince); subscribe() and unsubscribe() throw a bare C string. VS Code's
file watcher always subscribes, and a C string escapes the std::exception
catch blocks in Backend.cc, so on AIX the extension host died with SIGABRT the
moment a folder was opened and file changes made outside the editor were never
picked up.

With BRUTE_FORCE_POLLING defined (our AIX gyp block does), subscribe() takes
an initial scan and starts one thread per watcher that rescans the tree every
PARCEL_WATCHER_POLL_INTERVAL_MS (default 2000, minimum 250; the wait stretches
to 3x the scan time on big trees) and diffs it with DirTree::getChanges, the
same machinery getEventsSince uses. A deleted root is reported as a delete
event for the root and polling stops (like the native backends); other errors
during a rescan are reported through
the event stream and polling continues. Without the define the stubs now throw
std::runtime_error, so a missing backend is an error, not a crash.

Applies to @parcel/watcher v2.5.x.

